een the  states these interstate conflicts could  redefine how much sovereignty states  have in this federalist system and then  there are the trigger laws 13 states had  trigger laws on the books that banned  abortion as soon as roe and casey were  overturned these 13 states would trigger  laws had exceptions to save the life of  the pregnant woman but only five of  these states have exceptions for rape or  incest and if someone becomes pregnant  by a male relative in arkansas kentucky  louisiana missouri north and south  dakota tennessee texas or mississippi  they will be forced to give birth and if  someone is raped in those states they  will also be forced to give birth i just  have never believed that that having a  tragedy or a tragic situation happened  to someone is a reason to have another  tragedy occur so no exception that we  continue to make sure we go forward  there are five states including texas  where abortion is banned and there are  eight states where abortion will soon be  banned and there are of course a huge  variety of different pro-choice and  anti-abortion laws throughout the  country different states have different  cutoffs some are based on viability  others on a number of weeks  some allow exceptions for rape and  incest and some do not and it's only  going to get more complicated if states  rush to ban or allow abortion but what  about the fetal heartbeat bills until  the dom's ruling federal courts had  unanimously struck down the so-called  heartbeat bills outlawing abortion after  the detection of a fetal heartbeat and  that's why states like ohio georgia  south carolina and iowa passed trigger  laws with six-week abortion bans  legislatures did not repeal those laws  and abortion is banned after the sixth  week of pregnancy in those states the  supreme court decision did not say that  the states had to allow abortion until  week six so any of those states are free  to pass total bans if they desire though  the georgia law is currentl