 cannot  regulate and a state is likely to defend  such lawsuits by arguing that congress  did not give the fda explicit authority  to regulate abortion pills and thus uh  there's nothing to override state laws  and then there's interstate travel will  women be able to travel to another state  to get an abortion well justice  kavanaugh wrote in his concurring  opinion that the state cannot ban women  from traveling from one state to another  to get an abortion he called that an  easy question to answer however that  issue wasn't before the court in the  dobbs case and his opinion doesn't have  any legal weight so is he right well  there's actually not a lot of case law  on the right to travel between states  during the dust bowl years california  tried to ban immigration of people from  oklahoma at the time california asserted  that mass migration of indigent people  stressed the state's budget and created  a crisis of health and morals the states  generally have broad police power to  regulate health safety welfare and  morals of its citizens but the supreme  court struck down the law on the basis  that it was not a valid exercise of the  police power of california and that it  imposes an unconstitutional burden upon  interstate commerce in addition to the  commerce clause people might look to the  privileges and immunities clause which  protects the rights of u.s citizens  against discriminatory treatment under  the law of a different state and in a  1985 case called the supreme court of  new hampshire versus piper the u.s  supreme court held that a new hampshire  rule that limited bard mission to state  residents violated the privileges and  immunities clause  in 1999 the court decided another  privileges immunities case signs versus  roe in that case the court said that the  right to interstate travel consists of  three components one the right to enter  and leave another state two the right to  be treated as a welcome visitor when  temporarily president in another state  and three the right to be treated like  other citizens of that state for those  travelers who elect to become permanent  residents  these cases generally say that people  have a constitutional right to move  freely between states but states are  asserting new powers over their  residents we've covered the senate bill  8 the texas bounty hunter law that  allows citizens to sue anyone who  assists someone in obtaining an abortion  and since the supreme court didn't  strike that law down other states were  inspired to draft similar laws  in missouri lawmakers are debating a law  that would allow the state to criminally  prosecute anyone who aids someone in  getting an abortion in missouri but also  if they help the person travel to a  state where the abortion is legal and  obviously there are all sorts of  problems underlying these proposed laws  because it purports to make a criminal  conspiracy out of conduct that occurs  wholly in another state where missouri  has no jurisdiction and the  missouri legislators who are drafting  the bill also want to create civil  liability for anyone in the chain who  helps a missouri resident get an  abortion including people who do the  marketing of an out-of-state abortion  clinic though the supreme court has  weighed in on a similar situation before  in 1975 the court reversed the  conviction of a virginia editor whose  newspaper advertised reproductive health  services offered in new york the court  said quote a state does not acquire the  power or supervision over the internal  affairs in another state merely because  the welfare and health of its own  citizens may be affected when they  travel to that state now states that are  protective of reproductive rights like  california and massachusetts are also  passing laws welcoming non-residents who  want abortion services and shielding  their own citizens from prosecution and  civil liability for example a  massachusetts law exempts in-state  health care providers from