ican congress  impose a national ban on abortion if  they take power  well in doms the supreme court implied  that there was no due process right to  an abortion that leaves decisions up to  the state but we live in a federal  system with the supremacy clause so  could congress pass a national law that  banned abortion everywhere in the united  states  well a majority of the court probably  does not think the national abortion ban  would be unconstitutional in other words  they could probably get away with it if  congress enacted a national ban congress  could cite their power to regulate  interstate commerce under the commerce  clause which is often how many different  laws are passed but that would be pretty  easy to do congress could write a law  that says abortions can't be performed  if they use any device which traveled in  interstate commerce or using any  medication containing any component  which has traveled in interstate  commerce or simply that people being  born implicates commerce because they're  going to engage in commerce when they  get older or their parents engage in  commerce  as part of the process although many of  the court's conservative justices have  been skeptical of the government's  commerce power this situation could be  different if what's being regulated is  abortion justice roberts is the only  conservative justice on the court who  has supported a federal government's  broad right to regulate interstate  commerce so his fellow justices could  wind up lobbying him to uphold a ban  they could also invoke section 5 of the  14th amendment the due process clause  this of course is the same clause that  women once relied on for the right to  abortion but congress could pass a law  stating that fetuses are people under  the 14th amendment and that they have a  right to life under that section the  second question is could a democratic  congress  pass a nationwide pro-abortion law  making abortion legal in 50 states  well if congress enacted the law  codifying the right to an abortion  nationwide what would happen  again that would depend on the way that  the law is written and whether the court  would interpret the commerce power to  encompass such a law but here i would be  more skeptical the supreme court could  take a narrow interpretation of commerce  power as we just talked about and it's  entirely possible that this court is so  outcome driven that it would allow  commerce power for a ban on abortion but  would not allow commerce power for a  nationwide right to abortion but you  can't know until you try congress could  also state that women have a due process  liberty interest to an abortion uh that  would be advisory to the supreme court  they would be the ultimate arbiters but  that would put the ball back with the  supreme court again we can guess how the  supreme court might find on that  particular theory but again we don't  know and then there is the question of  all the other rights that are implicated  by substantive due process could the  supreme court start rolling back a lot  of the rights that we've come to presume  over the last 50 years  and the answer is very possibly those  rights have a target on their back  contraception interracial marriage  intimate sexual acts same-sex marriage  all stem from a common judicial doctrine  known as substantive due process  texas attorney general ken paxton says  he wants to start enforcing the state's  antiquated sodomy laws which are  unconstitutional under the current  supreme court jurisprudence but justice  thomas explicitly says that we should do  away with all substantive due process  rights justice kavanaugh says that dobbs  should have zero effect on other rights  that originate in substantive due  process  but the majority in dom suggests that  abortion is different because it  implicates potential life but also the  court engaged in a deeply rooted  analysis that only looks to history and  it's hard to see how any of those other  ri