her state  and three the right to be treated like  other citizens of that state for those  travelers who elect to become permanent  residents  these cases generally say that people  have a constitutional right to move  freely between states but states are  asserting new powers over their  residents we've covered the senate bill  8 the texas bounty hunter law that  allows citizens to sue anyone who  assists someone in obtaining an abortion  and since the supreme court didn't  strike that law down other states were  inspired to draft similar laws  in missouri lawmakers are debating a law  that would allow the state to criminally  prosecute anyone who aids someone in  getting an abortion in missouri but also  if they help the person travel to a  state where the abortion is legal and  obviously there are all sorts of  problems underlying these proposed laws  because it purports to make a criminal  conspiracy out of conduct that occurs  wholly in another state where missouri  has no jurisdiction and the  missouri legislators who are drafting  the bill also want to create civil  liability for anyone in the chain who  helps a missouri resident get an  abortion including people who do the  marketing of an out-of-state abortion  clinic though the supreme court has  weighed in on a similar situation before  in 1975 the court reversed the  conviction of a virginia editor whose  newspaper advertised reproductive health  services offered in new york the court  said quote a state does not acquire the  power or supervision over the internal  affairs in another state merely because  the welfare and health of its own  citizens may be affected when they  travel to that state now states that are  protective of reproductive rights like  california and massachusetts are also  passing laws welcoming non-residents who  want abortion services and shielding  their own citizens from prosecution and  civil liability for example a  massachusetts law exempts in-state  health care providers from liability for  providing services to abortion seekers  from out of state the law prohibits the  state from extraditing anyone to a state  that outlaws abortion and protects  people from having their medical records  disclosed to states  that have criminal penalties for  abortion but just because the supreme  court weighed in on a similar question  45 years ago doesn't mean that the  current supreme court is going to have  the same outcome especially where we're  going to have even more of a patchwork  of different laws some of which are  trying to create criminal guilt and some  of which are trying to prevent it and  then of course there is the first  amendment since abortion will be a crime  in several states some anti-abortion  advocates are already suggesting that  states outlaw any speech that provides  information that might be used to  terminate a pregnancy or encourages an  illegal abortion and there are a million  thorny questions like this that are  going to come up and  it's unknown how the courts are going to  treat these cases and then there is the  district of columbia washington dc is  not a state so now that the court has  sent abortion back to the states what  does that mean for dc residents well  washington dc is a federal district with  limited self-governance under the 1973  home rule act however under the  constitution congress is the ultimate  authority over washington dc so it's  likely that the current democratic  congress is not going to do anything to  restrict abortion in dc where it remains  legal today however a republican  congress might want to end that in fact  representative andrew clyde a republican  from georgia said his top focus will now  be quote ending dc's failed experiment  of home rule once and for all if  republicans retake congressional  majorities later this year they might  decide to criminalize abortion in dc and  that takes us to congressional action  there are two questions before congress  the first is could a republican congress  impose a national ban on abortion if  they take power  well in doms the supreme court implied  that there was no due process right to  an abortion that leaves decisions up to  the state but we live in a federal  system with the supremacy clause so  could congress pass a national law that  banned abortion everywhere in the united  states  well a majority of the court probably  does not think the national abortion ban  would be unconstitutional in other words  they could probably get away with it if  congress enacted a national ban congress  could cite their power to regulate  interstate commerce under the commerce  clause which is often how many different  laws are passed but that would be pretty  easy to do congress could write a law  that says abortions can't be performed  if they use any device which traveled in  interstate commerce or using any  medication containing any component  which has traveled in interstate  commerce or simply that people being  born implicates commerce because they're  going to engage in commerce when they  get older or their parents engage in  commerce  as part of the process although many of  the court's conservative justices have  been skeptical of the government's  commerce power this situation could be  different if what's being regulated is  abortion justice roberts is the only  conservative justice on the court who  has supported a federal government's  broad right to regulate interstate  commerce so his fellow justices could  wind up lobbying him to uphold a ban  they could also invoke section 5 of the  14th amendment the due process clause  this of course is the same clause that  women once relied on for the right to  abortion but congress could pass a law  stating that fetuses are people under  the 14th amendment and that they have a  right to life under that section the  second question is could a democratic  congress  pass a nationwide pro-abortion law  making abortion legal in 50 states  well if congress enacted the law  codifying the right to an abortion  nationwide what would happen  again that would depend on the way that  the law is written and whether the court  would interpret the commerce power to  encompass such a law but here i would be  more skeptical the supreme court could  take a narrow interpretation of commerce  power as we just talked about and it's  entirely possible that this court is so  outcome driven that it would allow  commerce power for a ban on abortion but  would not allow commerce power for a  nationwide right to abortion but you  can't know until you try congress could  also state that women have a due process  liberty interest to an abortion uh that  would be advisory to the supreme court  they would be the ultimate arbiters but  that would put the ball back with the  supreme court again we can guess how the  supreme court might find on that  particular theory but again we don't  know and then there is the question of  all the other rights that are implicated  by substantive due process could the  supreme court start rolling back a lot  of the rights that we've come to presume  over the last 50 years  and the answer is very possibly those  rights have a target on their back  contraception interracial marriage  intimate sexual acts same-sex marriage  all stem from a common judicial doctrine  known as substantive due process  texas attorney general ken paxton says  he wants to start enforcing the state's  antiquated sodomy laws which are  unconstitutional under the current  supreme court jurisprudence but justice  thomas explicitly says that we should do  away with all substantive due process  rights justice kavanaugh says that dobbs  should have zero effect on other rights  that originate in substantive due  process  but the majority in dom suggests that  abortion is different because it  implicates potential life but also the  court engaged in a deeply rooted  analysis that only looks to history and  it's hard to see how any of those other  rights would survive using the same  analysis that the court engaged in in  dobbs and the descent in dubs made this  crystal clear quote and no one should be  confident that this majority is done  with its work the right rowan casey  recognizes not stand alone to the  contrary the court has linked it for  decades to other settled freedoms  involving bodily integrity familial  relationships and procreation most  obviously the right to terminated  pregnancy arose out of the right to  purchase and use contraception in turn  those rights led more recently to rights  of same-sex intimacy and marriage they  are all part of the same constitutional  fabric protecting autonomous decision  making over the most personal of life  decisions the majority or to be more  accurate most of it is eager to tell us  today that nothing it does cast doubt on  the precedents that do not concern  abortion but how could that be the lone  rationale for what the majority does  today is that the right to elect an  abortion is not deeply rooted in history  not until roe the majority argues did  people think abortion fell within the  constitution's guarantee of liberty the  same could be said though of most of the  rights the majority claims it is not  tampering with the majority could write  just as long an opinion showing for  example that until the mid-20th century  there was no support in american law for  a constitutional right to obtain  contraceptives so one of two things must  be true either the majority does not  really believe in its own reasoning or  if it does all rights that have no  history stretching back to the mid-19th  century are insecure either the mass of  the majority's opinion is hypocrisy or  additional constitutional rights are  under threat it is one or the other so  don't be surprised if this supreme court  starts rolling back these freedoms and  rights and it would probably start with  same-sex marriage and all of that is why  there are a lot of reproductive health  charities out there that are going to  need some help and one very small way  that i like to do so  is a company that i work with called tab  for cause which lets you raise money for  charity just by opening up tabs and  browsing the internet and that includes  reproductive rights charities because  they just launched tab for reproductive  health  well because of all of this and that  lets you raise money for non-profit  organizations protecting and providing  access to reproductive health care like  the center for reproductive rights and  planned parenthood i know this is just a  little thing but it's something that you  can do right now from the comfort of  your own home the way it works is the  tab for cause is a browser extension  that allows you to pick the charity of  your choice and then every time you open  up a new tab instead of displaying a  blank page it displays a customizable  tab with a couple of tiny ads and the  money that you generate is then given to  the charity that you choose you can see  in real time how much money is being  generated and you can even customize  this tab in the way that you would like  it and legal eagle tabbers have raised  over 35 000 alone and i know this  because tab for causes finances are 100  transparent so you can see for yourself  how much they've raised and where it's  gone so if that sounds interesting to  you if you'd like to make a small  difference together all you have to do  is click on the link that's on screen  right now or in the description to  install the browser extension and then  that's it then every page that you open  will create revenue that goes to charity  in this case reproductive health  charities so all you have to do is click  on the link that's on screen or feel  free not to as well and until next time  i will see you in court
 

